<!--
  HEOL PlotJuggler layout — ANTON_HEOL / guidance_heol.c.

  WHY THIS EXISTS, AND NOT plotjuggler_mfc.xml
  ============================================
  guidance_heol.c reports on the SHARED GUIDANCE_MFC message (it is a generic
  per-axis SISO guidance record), so the MFC layout technically draws it. But
  one field's MEANING differs, and it is the field that breaks the plot:

      sp_traj_{x,y,z}  is  u_ff,  NOT a position setpoint trajectory.

  So on the MFC layout's shared XYZ axes, sp_traj_z (a THRUST, ~-7.8 N at
  hover) is drawn against sp_z / me_z (a POSITION, ~-2 m). Two different units
  on one y-axis: the position curves get squashed into a thin band near the
  top, and the thrust curve looks like a wild excursion below them. Nothing is
  wrong with the data — the axes are just meaningless together.

  This layout separates them. Every plot below holds ONE unit:

    Z: position [m]        sp_z / me_z / truth, and the residual err_z
    Z: thrust [N]          sp_traj_z (= u_ff = T*), cmd_z (total), fk_z
    XY: position [m]       sp_/me_/truth per axis
    XY: tilt cmd [rad]     sp_traj_ (= u_ff) and cmd_ per axis, plus fk_
                           BEWARE THE INDEX CONVENTION on this tab: the
                           horizontal channel is a 2x2 MIMO loop whose OUTPUT
                           space is (x,y) but whose INPUT space is (phi,theta).
                           cmd_x/sp_traj_x are heol_hxy.command[0]/u_ff[0] =
                           PHI [rad]; cmd_y/sp_traj_y are index 1 = THETA [rad].
                           They are NOT x/y accelerations. err_/me_/sp_ on the
                           other tabs ARE x/y [m]. So the correct pairing for
                           reading the loop is err_x (north) against cmd_y
                           (theta), and err_y (east) against cmd_x (phi);
                           the anti-diagonal alpha matrix is what crosses them.
                           fk_x/fk_y are genuine accelerations [m/s^2] in the
                           (x,y) output space, which is why they get their own
                           plot rather than sharing an axis with the commands.
    Nominal inputs         T*, phi*, theta* straight off the flat table, and
                           the Mx*/My*/Mz* nominal moments for the attitude
                           channels (SITL only, plumbing-check pair)
    Input sensitivity      alpha_z vs what the gz loop actually runs with,
                           and the four alpha_xy entries (telemetry-only)
    Attitude [rad]         sp_/me_/TRUTH per axis, roll/pitch/yaw tracking
    Attitude: torque [N*m] sp_traj_ (= u_ff) and cmd_ per axis, plus fk_,
                           same total-command-clamp reading as "Z: thrust"
    Motors [pprz]          u0..u3, the post-WLS actuator outputs

  READING "Z: thrust [N]" and "Attitude: torque [N*m]": cmd_* is clamped to
  the physical range (thrust: [-GZ_MAX_THRUST, 0], 0 = motors off, negative =
  up; torque: the per-axis max from stabilization_heol.c). If cmd_* is sitting
  ON its two rail values and switching between them, that loop is
  bang-banging, not tracking — check "Z: position" / "Attitude [rad]" before
  reading anything else on the run.

  Same transport as the MFC layout: one UDP-Server on port 9870, protocol
  JSON, timestamp field "timestamp". HEOL_NOMINAL/* and HEOL_ALPHA/* come from
  the NPS scope (SITL only); everything else draws on both feeds. Attitude
  tracking (sp_phi/me_phi/...) is drawn from MFC_STAB (renamed on relay from
  STAB_MFC, id 212 in messages.xml) — the SAME message shape stabilization_heol
  reuses for roll/pitch/yaw the way guidance_heol.c reuses GUIDANCE_MFC for
  x/y/z. sp_traj_phi/theta/psi is repurposed for u_ff, exactly like sp_traj_z.
-->
